From: yaegerbomb42 <yaeger.james42@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 19:47:41 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] docs(lenses): document the output lenses and assert the cache
 in e2e

The lens feature landed with PR #60; this tightens it up:

- README: an "Output lenses" section with the five lenses, the caps, the
  cheapest-model rule, the per-message+lens cache and the telemetry
  attribution, so the behaviour is discoverable in the app docs.
- e2e: picking the same lens on the same message must not reach the server
  a second time (the answer is cached per message + lens), and a different
  lens on the same message is a second, uncached answer.
---
 apps/swarmagents/README.md          | 35 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 apps/swarmagents/e2e/README.md      |  6 +++++
 apps/swarmagents/e2e/lenses.spec.ts | 17 ++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 58 insertions(+)

diff --git a/apps/swarmagents/README.md b/apps/swarmagents/README.md
index 3bcff7077..f27f4986a 100644
--- a/apps/swarmagents/README.md
+++ b/apps/swarmagents/README.md
@@ -203,6 +203,41 @@ policy error rather than hanging forever).
 - The risk classifier is side-effect free: it never runs, reads, or resolves
   anything; unit tests cover the policy table.
 
+### Output lenses (re-read any answer)
+
+Every agent message in the thread panel carries a **Lens** menu:
+
+| Lens | What it does |
+| --- | --- |
+| `ELI5` | The same answer, explained in plain words for a curious 5-year-old. |
+| `Executive summary` | What changed, why it matters and what to do next — under 120 words. |
+| `Code review` | Concrete issues only, each as `[blocker\|major\|minor] file:line — issue — fix`. |
+| `Security audit` | Findings as `[critical\|high\|medium\|low] issue — impact — fix`. |
+| `Architecture diagram` | One mermaid `flowchart`, rendered as a real diagram. |
+
+Picking a lens sends **that message** — plus the files the run touched, capped
+at 12 000 message characters and 5 files of 4 000 characters each — through a
+short specialised prompt and shows the answer **inline under the message**, in
+a collapsible panel that also shows the serving model and the cost of the
+call.
+
+- The lens runs on the **cheapest capable model** the user has: the `fast`
+  tier of their own providers, ordered by price with free providers first
+  (`lib/lenses/run.ts`). Keys never leave the server.
+- Answers are **cached per message + lens** in the user's own store
+  (`lenses.json`, next to their providers) and in the browser session, so the
+  same lens on the same message never costs a second provider call.
+- The architecture lens is rendered by a dependency-free mermaid renderer
+  (`lib/client/mermaid.ts` + `minimalist-ui/src/components/Mermaid.tsx`), so
+  the user sees a diagram, not the diagram's source.
+- The lens call is published on the agent run's telemetry bus with
+  `source: "lens"`, so its cost shows up in the telemetry drawer and in the
+  usage meter next to the run that produced the message.
+- `POST /api/lenses` is scoped to the caller (`x-swarm-user`), reads the
+  referenced files from that user's own VM, and has its own per-user rate
+  limit (`SWARM_LENS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN`, default 20). Cached answers are free
+  and never touch the rate limit.
+
 ### Idle hibernation of persistent VMs
 
 A persistent per-user VM holds CPU, memory, and a container slot even when
